AB  - Evidence-grounded question answering (QA) is critical in healthcare, where responses must be traceable to trusted sources and produced in a verifiable format. This paper presents a local, evidence-grounded healthcare QA pipeline built on the MedQuAD dataset, a collection of question-answer pairs curated from National Institutes of Health (NIH) resources. The pipeline retrieves a top- $\boldsymbol{k}$ candidate pool using a hybrid scoring function that combines lexical relevance (BM25) and latent semantic similarity (LSA), then selects a compact evidence subset of size mfor downstream generation. The main contribution is a costaware evidence subset selection method formulated as a Quadratic Unconstrained Binary Optimization (QUBO) objective that maximizes summed relevance while penalizing redundancy and a token-based cost proxy. Three selection strategies are compared: (i) a ranking baseline (Top- $m$), (ii) a relevance-diversity QUBO selector $(\gamma=0)$, and (iii) a cost-aware QUBO selector $(\gamma>0)$. A locally hosted LLM generates answers constrained to a strict JSON schema, enabling deterministic validation of output structure and evidence identifiers. Experimental results on representative healthcare queries show that the cost-aware QUBO reduces mean evidence cost and redundancy relative to Top- $\boldsymbol{m}$ while maintaining comparable relevance and full schema compliance. A trade-off analysis further highlights how optimization-based selection improves evidence efficiency with minimal impact on end-to-end latency, supporting practical local deployment of grounded healthcare QA systems.
