AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged as an effective approach for question answering over domain-specific documents by grounding large language model outputs in external knowledge sources. However, most existing RAG pipelines rely on fixed-size document chunking during retrieval, which may not be optimal for diverse query types commonly encountered in policy documents. In particular, questions related to mental health and well-being policies range from factual definitions to procedural and role-based inquiries, each requiring different levels of contextual information. In this paper, we propose a query-aware adaptive chunking strategy for RAG that dynamically selects chunk sizes based on the inferred query intent. Instead of modifying or fine-tuning the underlying language model, the proposed approach introduces a lightweight retrieval-time adaptation mechanism that aligns retrieved context with the informational needs of different query types. The method is evaluated on mental health and wellbeing policy documents published by the World Health Organization, focusing on workplace-related guidelines and recommendations. Evaluation is conducted at both the retrieval and generation levels. Retrieval performance is assessed using Hit@5 and answer coverage metrics, while generation quality is evaluated through manual human assessment using a 3-point Likert scale measuring relevancy, faithfulness, and completeness. Experimental results show that while fixed and adaptive RAG approaches achieve comparable retrieval performance, the adaptive strategy consistently improves generation quality, particularly for procedural and role-based policy questions. These findings demonstrate that query-aware adaptive chunking can enhance the effectiveness of RAG systems in healthcare policy question answering without requiring additional model training.
