AB  - To address the critical challenge of balancing computational cost and response quality in large language model (LLM) deployments, this paper introduces RAGRouter, an innovative routing strategy model based on Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG). The widespread application of LLMs in natural language generation, dialogue systems, and question-answering faces a fundamental trade-off: larger models yield higher quality but incur prohibitive costs, while smaller models often prove inadequate for complex queries. Existing routing strategies, though aiming to optimize resource efficiency via dynamic model selection, frequently suffer from misallocated resources and a mismatch between model capabilities and task demands, further limited by insufficient semantic understanding and underutilized syntactic features. Our proposed RAGRouter framework tackles these limitations by achieving accurate query difficulty assessment and efficient model selection through sophisticated feature fusion and knowledge enhancement. Specifically, the model extracts deep contextual semantic features and constructs syntactic representations from dependency parse trees. A cross-modal attention mechanism subsequently fuses these semantic and syntactic elements into a holistic query representation. Furthermore, the integration of RAG technology builds a vector knowledge base, which uses K-Nearest Neighbors retrieval to find historically similar queries and their routing labels, thereby generating context-enhanced prompts for fine-tuning the QWEN-7B model. To bolster generalization and address data imbalance, we also propose a data augmentation technique using semantic and structural transformations, complemented by a perplexity-based dynamic sampling strategy that employs weighted and oversampling methods to improve robustness on complex tasks. Experimental results on public benchmarks demonstrate that RAGRouter significantly enhances the accuracy and stability of routing decisions, substantially reducing the invocation rate of powerful models like GPT-4 while maintaining high response quality. The model outperforms state-of-the-art baselines, showing superior adaptability and generalization in mathematical reasoning and knowledge-intensive question-answering, thus providing a scalable and effective technical pathway for optimizing resource efficiency in practical LLM applications. The relevant code of the experiment has been uploaded to GitHub. The link is: https://github.com/thebluecitysky/Routing-Research.
ER  - 

