AB  - Engineering operation-and-maintenance (O&M) workflows accumulate large volumes of technical knowledge, including equipment manuals, technical standards, defect cases, maintenance records, and troubleshooting experience. However, these assets are often scattered across personal computers, paper archives, and heterogeneous business systems, making knowledge access slow and reuse inefficient. In practice, field engineers frequently issue fuzzy queries by describing symptoms, scenarios, or incomplete device names rather than using standardized technical terminology. To this end, we propose TraceRAG, a traceability-aware retrieval-augmented question answering framework for fuzzy-query answering over engineering knowledge bases. The framework integrates Query Alignment, hybrid retrieval with cross-encoder reranking, and evidence-grounded generation with per-stage trace logging. Query Alignment combines intent-oriented template expansion and LLM-based rewriting while preserving the original query to reduce semantic drift. Retrieved candidates are then fused and reranked before answer generation, and supporting evidence is explicitly exposed to improve answer transparency and reduce hallucination risk. We evaluate the method on a 1,131-item electrical engineering knowledge benchmark together with 678 synthetic fuzzy queries. The results show that cross-encoder reranking improves canonical-query MRR@10 by +0.082 absolute over hybrid retrieval, while Query Alignment with reranking achieves the best fuzzy-query MRR@10 of 0.660. These results indicate that TraceRAG improves fuzzy-query knowledge access on the reported benchmark and has potential relevance for O&Moriented technical knowledge retrieval and reuse.
ER  - 

