AB  - In the present paper, the comparative analysis of current Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) frameworks is provided with the main emphasis on effectiveness and safety of these tools in the context of the question-answer task in a private setting. RAG is a solution to the core problem of factual error in Large Language Models (LLMs) in order to base their responses on external, updated knowledge bases. Nevertheless, the fact that it can apply to sensitive, proprietary data raises a new, more serious issue: the very mechanism that makes RAG effective, the access to an outside knowledge base, introduces new security vulnerabilities. In this regard, we perform a thorough literature review of more than 20 recent scholarly articles, describing basic and state-of-the-art RAG structures. We suggest a multi-layered assessment framework including the usual performance-based measures (latency, throughput, accuracy) and the retrieval and generation quality-specific measures (Precision at k, Faithfulness, Hallucination scores). We critically evaluate the notable RAG systems, such as LangChain, LlamaIndex, and Haystack, in terms of architectural design, scalability, and the compatibility with privacy-sensitive deployments. We conduct a thorough evaluation of major privacy-protective methods, such as Differential Privacy (DP), Secure Multi-Party Computation (SMPC), and Homomorphic Encryption (HE) and measure privacy-utility trade-offs they present. The analysis points out that, although the specialized architectures such as DPSparseVoteRAG provide promising solutions, the enforcement of strong privacy guarantees can be very costly in terms of performance. It is a useful resource to developers and researchers interested in designing secure and performant RAG systems to support sensitive applications, giving a best practices overview and defining essential future research directions in privacy-preserving AI. The current work includes a comparative analysis of three popular RAG models, namely, LangChain, LlamaIndex, and Haystack based on their architectural appropriateness to private question answering and not on controlled experimental benchmarking. The scope of evaluation focuses on performance features, deployment, and privacy-sensitive integration features. The key results include a definite privacy-utility trade-off in which strict privacy assurances by mechanisms like Differential Privacy, Secure Multi-Party Computation and Homomorphic Encryption lead to quantifiable losses in latency, throughput or accuracy of the answers.
ER  - 

