AB  - Addressing the challenges of difficult access to overwhelming volumes of contingency plan knowledge and inaccurate retrieval results in existing power emergency plans, this study proposes a large language model (llM) and knowledge graph-based Retrieval-augmented Generation (RAG) methodology for power emergency knowledge question answering. The proposed method first leverages LLMs to analyze user queries, performing entity extraction and relation linking to construct graph query statements. Then, the retrieved knowledge and user queries serve as input to LLMs, which executes joint reasoning and problemsolving operations. Experiments on a power grid emergency plan for a large-area blackout event in a city show that the proposed method enables enhanced knowledge retrieval based on knowledge graphs and demonstrates strong question-answering capabilities for power emergency handling scenarios.
