AB  - Contemporary chatbots incorporating large language models (LLMs) facilitate access to complex government budget information. However, conventional Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) architectures remain inadequate for public finance systems requiring transparency and accountability, as they lack technically enforceable mechanisms to verify source document integrity. This paper proposes a Cryptographically Verifiable Retrieval-Augmented Generation (CV-RAG) architecture that incorporates blockchain anchoring and cryptographic integrity verification into conventional RAG. Each document segment is hashed and incorporated into a Merkle tree, with the root permanently recorded on a public blockchain. Upon receiving queries, the chatbot retrieves relevant segments and includes cryptographic proof enabling verification against on-chain records. A functional prototype for an Indonesian e-government budget chatbot, developed using Design Science Research methodology, demonstrates that CV-RAG introduces minimal latency overhead (<7% for core queries) while enabling sub-second user-verifiable integrity assessments. Results confirm feasibility of incorporating cryptographic verifiability into LLM-based public service chatbots.
