AB  - Multi-hop question answering represents one of the most challenging tasks for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems, requiring synthesis of information from multiple disconnected sources. This paper presents a systematic evaluation of three RAG architectures-vector-based (NaiveRAG), graph-based (Microsoft GraphRAG), and hybrid (LightRAG)specifically focused on multi-hop performance. Using 100 multihop questions derived from a post-training-cutoff literary corpus, we evaluate each system using semantic similarity metrics and LLM-based qualitative assessment. Results demonstrate that the hybrid approach (LightRAG) achieves superior performance with a composite score of 0.653 and LLM-as-a-Judge rating of 4.24/5, significantly outperforming both vector-based ($0.542,2.92$) and graph-based (0.551, 2.89) systems. Analysis reveals that entity consolidation and dual-level retrieval are critical factors enabling effective multi-hop reasoning.
