AB  - Power-system technical standards provide the engineering basis for substation design, equipment acceptance, operation and maintenance, relay protection, and grid security control. Large language models (LLMs) offer a convenient interface to this knowledge, but their answers may be unreliable when requirements vary with equipment type, voltage level, operating condition, numerical limit, physical unit, or clause scope. This paper proposes TSAC-RAG, a type- and structure-aware retrieval-augmented generation framework for question answering over power-system standards. It combines lexical relevance, numerical–unit consistency, clause hierarchy, and question-type information to retrieve and rerank applicable clauses. The evidence is then supplied to an instruction-tuned LLM for engineering interpretation and citation-grounded answer generation. An audit of EleQA identifies 31,181 nonempty clauses and 18,584 questions with exact evidence mappings. Across three balanced test samples, TSAC-RAG achieves 83.82 ± 0.54% Recall@1, 96.81 ± 0.03% Recall@10, and 88.73 ± 0.28% MRR@10, improving over the strongest unconstrained retriever by 1.31, 0.50, and 1.11 percentage points. The results show that power-domain constraints provide LLMs with more applicable and traceable evidence. By improving the accuracy and traceability of standard-clause retrieval, TSAC-RAG can support safer and more reliable intelligent assistance for power-system design, equipment acceptance, operation and maintenance, relay protection, and grid security control.
