AB  - Applying large language models (LLMs) to time series question answering (TSQA) requires effective fusion of continuous temporal data with discrete language representations. However, existing approaches face three persistent challenges: quantization errors in numerical modeling, misalignment between temporal indices and data values, and opaque reasoning processes that increase hallucination risks. This paper introduces HiTS, a multimodal framework that addresses these challenges through three integrated mechanisms: (1) Layer-wise Temporal Token Injection (LTI), which extracts hierarchical features from a pre-trained time series encoder and injects them across multiple LLM layers for fine-grained numerical representation; (2) Geometry-Guided Visual Prompting (GVP), which maps query-specified temporal intervals to precise visual anchors in charts for spatial-temporal alignment; and (3) an Adaptive Parse–Render–Fuse (APRF) reasoning workflow that decomposes complex queries into structured sub-tasks with explicit evidence grounding. Experiments on diverse TSQA benchmarks show that HiTS consistently outperforms models with significantly larger parameter counts, including Qwen3-VL-30B and DeepSeek-V3.2, demonstrating the effectiveness of hierarchical multimodal fusion for time series understanding.
ER  - 

