AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed across edge and cloud platforms for real-time question-answering (QA) and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). Processing lengthy contexts in such distributed systems incurs high computational, memory, and bandwidth cost. We introduce a semantic caching approach that differs from existing KV-cache techniques: rather than caching low-level key-value tensors (which require model-internal access), we cache textual intermediate summaries compatible with black-box LLM APIs. Unlike prior semantic caching that stores end-to-end query-answer pairs, we target intermediate stages in multi-step RAG workflows, enabling reuse across semantically related queries. The method reduces redundant computation by 50-60% while preserving answer quality on NaturalQuestions, TriviaQA, and a synthetic ArXiv dataset, balancing cost and quality for real-time AI assistants in cloud and edge deployments.
ER  - 

